WHAT A SURVEY OF THESE REPOSITORIES REPORTED. These are LEADS, not settled facts.

A single pass swept the whole estate with limited tools, and it has been wrong: on
2026-08-08 it reported that a repository contained no reference to a package that its
own source uses in twenty files, and a roster was minted on that. Treat every line
below as what the survey BELIEVES, to be confirmed by the investigator that owns the
codeline. Do NOT restate any of it in a brief as established, verified, or confirmed —
a brief is inherited whole and re-checked by nothing, so a wrong lead written as a fact
becomes an instruction.

Propose roles for the codelines the work appears to REACH. A codeline reported
no_work_found is not confirmed clear, and one reported not_investigated or failed was
not established either way — do not treat any of the three as proof about that repo:

- gotransit: in_scope — areas: src/components/pages/CheckoutPage
    files it opened: src/components/pages/CheckoutPage/CheckoutForm.tsx, src/components/pages/CheckoutPage/types.ts, src/components/pages/CheckoutPage/__tests__/CheckoutForm.spec.tsx
    evidence: Searched for confirmEmail/checkout email references and found src/components/pages/CheckoutPage/CheckoutForm.tsx, which renders the 'email' and 'confirmEmail' FormInput fields. The confirmEmail field's validate rule (line 305-309) does `if (email && value && value !== email) { return t("TRM774"); }` — a case-sensitive strict string comparison, exactly matching the ticket's description of the Go-Checkout page flagging same-email-different-casing as a mismatch. TRM774 is the dictionary term referenced in one of the linked ticket documents. Also opened types.ts confirming ICheckoutFormValues has email/confirmEmail as plain strings, and CheckoutForm.spec.tsx which has an existing test 'should trigger validation error on blur only when confirmEmail field is not empty' exercising this validation path with getEmail()/getConfirmEmail() helpers.

SEPARATELY — and this is a recommendation about the TEAM, not something discovered
about the code: a survey of the estate suggests these codelines need their own
investigator. Propose one per codeline named here, with the stated focus:

- gotransit: focus on Confirm whether any other email-comparison logic exists beyond CheckoutForm.tsx (e.g. server-side/API validation of email/confirmEmail on submit) that would also need case-insensitive treatment, and check EMAIL_REGEX/EMAIL_INPUT_MAX_LENGTH constants for any casing-related assumptions. (because The bug site found in CheckoutForm.tsx is a client-side react-hook-form validate rule; there may be additional validation (e.g. in an API route or shared checkout schema) that duplicates this comparison and was not fully surveyed.)

AND — also a recommendation about the TEAM — the survey reports these codelines need
work WRITTEN, not merely read. An investigator cannot change code; every story is
assigned to an implementer, so propose at least one implementer that can build this:

- gotransit: must be able to write Change the confirmEmail validate rule's equality check in CheckoutForm.tsx to compare emails case-insensitively (e.g. normalize casing before comparing), and update/add test coverage in CheckoutForm.spec.tsx for mixed-case matching emails passing validation and genuinely different emails still failing. (because CheckoutForm.tsx:306 contains the exact strict `value !== email` comparison causing the reported case-sensitivity bug, and CheckoutForm.spec.tsx already has an existing test suite structure for this validation path that a fix should extend.)
